action and method are the browser's; hx-post and hx-target are HTMX's. The URL is the same in both, so one handler answers both and branches on the header. Chapter 1 follows this form through both modes.

D.4 Inline edit
The pattern the book uses as its worked example is a row that becomes a form and back. The Edit control is a form so that it has a plain path.


src/main/resources/templates/tasks/_item.peb

    &lt;form action="/tasks" method="get"
          hx-get="/tasks/{{ task.id }}/edit" hx-target="#task-{{ task.id }}" hx-swap="outerHTML"&gt;
      &lt;input type="hidden" name="edit" value="{{ task.id }}"&gt;
      &lt;button type="submit" class="secondary" aria-describedby="task-title-{{ task.id }}"&gt;Edit&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;

The browser GETs /tasks?edit=&lt;id&gt; and gets the list page with that row as a form; HTMX GETs /tasks/&lt;id&gt;/edit and gets the form on its own, which replaces the &lt;li&gt; because the target is the row and the swap is outerHTML.


src/main/resources/templates/tasks/_edit.peb

&lt;li id="task-{{ task.id }}" class="task task-editing"&gt;
  &lt;form action="/tasks/{{ task.id }}/edit" method="post"
        hx-post="/tasks/{{ task.id }}/edit" hx-target="#task-{{ task.id }}" hx-swap="outerHTML"&gt;
    &lt;label for="title-{{ task.id }}"&gt;Title&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" id="title-{{ task.id }}" name="title" value="{{ editTitle }}" autofocus
           aria-describedby="hint-{{ task.id }}{% if editError %} error-{{ task.id }}{% endif %}"{% if editError %} aria-invalid="true"{% endif %}&gt;
    &lt;small id="hint-{{ task.id }}"&gt;Between {{ minTitleLength }} and {{ maxTitleLength }} characters.&lt;/small&gt;
    {% if editError %}&lt;p id="error-{{ task.id }}" class="error-message" role="alert"&gt;{{ editError }}&lt;/p&gt;{% endif %}
    &lt;div class="task-actions"&gt;
      &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;a href="/tasks" hx-get="/tasks/{{ task.id }}/view" hx-target="#task-{{ task.id }}" hx-swap="outerHTML"&gt;Cancel&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;

The form has the same &lt;li&gt; id as the row it replaced, so the response to Save, which is the row again, swaps back over it. autofocus on the field is honoured by HTMX when it swaps the content in, and by the browser on a full page load. Cancel is a plain link to the list with an hx-get for the view row. The handler is short because the templates carry the pattern.


src/main/kotlin/routes/TaskRoutes.kt

/** HTMX gets the edit form in place of the item; without JavaScript the list page opens with that task in edit mode. */
private suspend fun ApplicationCall.editTask(store: TaskStore) {
    val task = store.getById(parameters["id"].orEmpty()) ?: return respond(HttpStatusCode.NotFound)
    if (isHtmxRequest()) {
        respondHtml(renderTemplate("tasks/_edit.peb", editContext(task, task.title)))
    } else {
        seeOther(tasksPath(edit = task.id))
    }
}

